unds Department of Defense military construction, family housing, and related defense infrastructure projects."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Military construction and family housing appropriations create direct demand for construction contractors and engineering firms."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill appropriates funds for Department of Agriculture programs, rural development, and farm-related services."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill funds Department of Veterans Affairs healthcare services, which are delivered through VA hospitals and health systems."},{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"FDA appropriations affect the agency's capacity to review drugs, medical devices, and regulate pharmaceutical products."}],"plainSummary":"This bill provides fiscal year 2026 funding for three major areas: military construction and veterans' programs, agriculture and food safety agencies, and the operations of Congress. It allocates money to the Department of Defense for building projects and housing, the Department of Veterans Affairs for healthcare and benefits, the Department of Agriculture and FDA for farm programs and food regulation, and the Senate and congressional support agencies. The bill also sets conditions on how these funds may be spent.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who pays tax or gets a federal payment.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"C001051","score":0.030711644548022634},"overlapCount":1},"meaning":{"percentile":86.76447389178104,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[{"kind":"sponsor","text":"They wrote this bill. Sponsoring a bill about your own district's industry is the ordinary case, not the odd one."},{"kind":"state-industry","text":"Defense & Aerospace is also the top donor sector for 5 other members from TX. That points to the industry being big in the state, not to anything about this member."}],"unattributedShare":0.6745228249221403}},{"billId":"119-hr-7","bioguideId":"C001051","cycle":2026,"score":0.014274604011987088,"matches":[{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","donorAmount":25500,"donorShare":0.02006932164063698,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.005902741659010877},{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":18500,"donorShare":0.014560096092226828,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.00485336536407561},{"industry":"insurance","donorAmount":12000,"donorShare":0.009444386654417401,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0035184969889006006}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6745228249221403},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-7","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"7","title":"No Taxpayer Funding for Abortion and Abortion Insurance Full Disclosure Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committees on the Judiciary, and Ways and Means, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Appropriations","Assault and harassment offenses","Comprehensive health care","Consumer affairs","Crimes against women","Employee benefits and pensions","Government buildings, facilities, and property","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Health facilities and institutions","Health programs administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000522","cosponsorCount":109,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/7","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly restricts what health insurance plans may cover and how federal subsidies interact with abortion coverage in qualified health plans."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill prohibits federal health care facilities and federal employees from providing abortion services, affecting facilities that currently receive federal funds."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Organizations focused on reproductive health policy and abortion access or restrictions have a direct stake in the regulatory framework this bill establishes."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make permanent the restrictions on federal funding for abortion services and health insurance coverage that includes abortion. Currently, these restrictions are renewed each year through appropriations bills; the bill would apply them across all federal programs and funds. It would also prohibit qualified health plans offered through insurance marketplaces from covering abortion. The bill includes exceptions for cases of rape, incest, or when the pregnant person's life is in danger.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys health insurance. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"B001260","score":0.061260165720508956},"overlapCount":107},"meaning":{"percentile":60.89288531314088,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6745228249221403}},{"billId":"119-hr-8205","bioguideId":"C001051","cycle":2026,"score":0.008467883713297084,"matches":[{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":18500,"donorShare":0.014560096092226828,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.004314102545844986},{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":7500,"donorShare":0.005902741659010877,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.004153781167452098}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6745228249221403},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-8205","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"8205","title":"Accelerating Access to Critical Therapies for ALS Reauthorization Act of 2026","introducedDate":"2026-04-06","latestActionDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate. Read twice. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 466.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Department of Health and Human Services","Drug therapy","Food and Drug Administration (FDA)","Government information and archives","Health programs administration and funding","Internet, web applications, social media","Medical research","National Institutes of Health (NIH)","Neurological disorders","Public-private cooperation","Research administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"Q000023","cosponsorCount":102,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/8205","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly funds research grants and regulatory pathways for development of drugs and therapies targeting ALS and neurodegenerative diseases."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.4,"rationale":"Providers treating ALS patients may see changes in available investigational therapies and clinical trial access, though the bill focuses primarily on research and regulatory processes."}],"plainSummary":"This bill extends funding through fiscal year 2031 for federal programs that support research and development of treatments for ALS (Lou Gehrig's disease) and similar neurodegenerative diseases. It requires the National Institutes of Health to track safety data on experimental drugs and mandates that th